{
  "case_id": "2008-07-1122",
  "document_hash": "81f57b4e35a91ef51e80afc479a66b010b40d0622231c06211e8476d3366f7a0",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Joseph L. Parkhurst (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1122",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "81f57b4e35a91ef51e80afc479a66b010b40d0622231c06211e8476d3366f7a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-81f57b4e35a9-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Petitioner asks that this Court apply the principles established in Pennsylvania v. Mimms to uphold the pat-down search of a vehicle passenger seized during a lawful traffic stop. Mimms established that a traffic stop satisfies Terry's first prong as to suspicion of criminal activity, and it also established that a pat down of a driver is justified if the officer has a reasonable suspicion that the driver is armed and dangerous."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1122",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "81f57b4e35a91ef51e80afc479a66b010b40d0622231c06211e8476d3366f7a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-81f57b4e35a9-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "-- in your opening. Did you say that Mimms established that it's likely that he's armed and dangerous? I missed that."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1122",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "81f57b4e35a91ef51e80afc479a66b010b40d0622231c06211e8476d3366f7a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-81f57b4e35a9-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Do you agree that there's a point in an interaction that begins with a traffic stop, begins with a seizure, at which the nature of that interaction is changed so that it's no longer -- so that it becomes a consensual interaction?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Toby J. Heytens (United States, amicus curiae)",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1122",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "81f57b4e35a91ef51e80afc479a66b010b40d0622231c06211e8476d3366f7a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-81f57b4e35a9-0000-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The decision in this case should be reversed for one of two independent reasons. First, at the time that Officer Trevizo performed this frisk, the Arizona Court of Appeals erred when it held that the seizure that had been caused by the initial traffic stop was already over. This seizure was still ongoing at the time of the frisk."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Heytens",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1122",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "81f57b4e35a91ef51e80afc479a66b010b40d0622231c06211e8476d3366f7a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-81f57b4e35a9-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We would say that the fundamental reality of this situation is what the Court recognized in Brendlin, which is that at the time the car pulls over a reasonable person would understand himself to not be free to leave unless and until the police officer says something that makes fairly clear that the seizure is over and he's free to go."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1122",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "81f57b4e35a91ef51e80afc479a66b010b40d0622231c06211e8476d3366f7a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-81f57b4e35a9-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Let me ask you, suppose it's a roadside encounter in which the driver is changing a flat tire and the officer stops and wants to talk to him for a while and then he suddenly thinks, well, maybe this guy's armed. Is it okay for him to go ahead and search him?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Pincus",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1122",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "81f57b4e35a91ef51e80afc479a66b010b40d0622231c06211e8476d3366f7a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-81f57b4e35a9-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. PINCUS: It is subjective. And I guess I would also point, Your Honor, to the Court's decision in Drayton, which dealt with the question in a somewhat different context, at a bus search, and whether or not passengers on a bus would feel that they were seized when there were officers in the front of the bus and they were asking questions."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1122",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "81f57b4e35a91ef51e80afc479a66b010b40d0622231c06211e8476d3366f7a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-81f57b4e35a9-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: And then don't you lose under that line of cases? This is the picture that the officer was painting, or I thought was, to bring this within the \"Mind if I search your luggage\" category of cases."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Pincus",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1122",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "81f57b4e35a91ef51e80afc479a66b010b40d0622231c06211e8476d3366f7a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-81f57b4e35a9-0002-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. PINCUS: Well, I guess a couple of answers to that, Justice Breyer. First of all, if this is just a case about whether the lower court made a correct determination about whether there was a seizure or not, there's not -- maybe there's not much to the case. We think the lower court did make the right determination for the reasons that I gave."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Pincus",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1122",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "81f57b4e35a91ef51e80afc479a66b010b40d0622231c06211e8476d3366f7a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-81f57b4e35a9-0002-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. PINCUS: Well, Your Honor, we think if -- if there was a seizure, then -- then we think there is a -- a second question, which is even if the person was seized, it's not clear to us that the Court's precedents say that -- that that automatically permits a pat down on armed and dangerousness."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1122",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "81f57b4e35a91ef51e80afc479a66b010b40d0622231c06211e8476d3366f7a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-81f57b4e35a9-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 54,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: I guess what about -- I -- I guess if we held that you could do this, this pat-down search here, it would probably carry forward to any other kind of seizure like a -- a roadblock to inspect for drunken driving or anything like that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1122",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "81f57b4e35a91ef51e80afc479a66b010b40d0622231c06211e8476d3366f7a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-81f57b4e35a9-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "You -- you don't concede in this case, or do you, that Trevizo had a reasonable suspicion that Johnson was armed and dangerous?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1122",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "81f57b4e35a91ef51e80afc479a66b010b40d0622231c06211e8476d3366f7a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-81f57b4e35a9-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Do you argue in -- in all of this that this guy was not dangerous?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1122",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "81f57b4e35a91ef51e80afc479a66b010b40d0622231c06211e8476d3366f7a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-81f57b4e35a9-0003-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We -- we agree. We argue very strenuously that he wasn't dangerous, but -- but I -- I think the key point here is -- is that it -- it may well be a sliver of cases, but removing the sort of mutually reinforcing nature of these two tests threatens to really downgrade the standard in a way that officer discretion --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1122",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "81f57b4e35a91ef51e80afc479a66b010b40d0622231c06211e8476d3366f7a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-81f57b4e35a9-0003-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 60,
        "start": 60
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "REBUTTAL ARGUMENT OF JOSEPH L. PARKHURST ON BEHALF OF THE PETITIONER"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1122",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "81f57b4e35a91ef51e80afc479a66b010b40d0622231c06211e8476d3366f7a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-81f57b4e35a9-0003-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 60,
        "start": 60
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "This -- this case falls directly under Mimms and Wilson. Mimms and Wilson both hold that the officer has the authority to control the occupants of an automobile with or without suspicion of any wrongdoing by the occupant."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1122",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "81f57b4e35a91ef51e80afc479a66b010b40d0622231c06211e8476d3366f7a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-81f57b4e35a9-0003-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 60,
        "start": 60
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What do you think?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1122",
      "confidence": 0.0,
